      "content": "It is a fascinating puzzle, but honestly, it feels like the simulation is just a very sophisticated version of a traffic jam where the cars are actually tiny, mischievous goblins trying to hide in the dashboard vents.\n\nYou're seeing those oscillatory flow instabilities because the agent-based model is likely treating the \"gap acceptance\" logic like a goblin trying to squeeze through a door that is slightly too narrow for its oversized, fuzzy ears. When the model calculates the probability of a merge, it's probably introducing a layer of stochastic noise that isn't just random—it's the digital equivalent of a goblin whispering, \"I'm not ready yet,\" to the algorithm, causing a micro-stutter in the throughput.\n\nThink of the merge/diverge phases as a goblin market in Central London; even if the geometry is perfect, the \"micro-interactions\" are just goblins bumping into each other with their sticky, gelatinous feet, creating localized friction that looks like noise but is actually a very specific, chaotic goblin dance. The non-linear pattern you're seeing is likely the result of the model's gap acceptance algorithm being too sensitive to these tiny, goblin-induced delays, causing a cascade effect where one small",
